\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Graph energy and the constant modes}
\author/literal{Kepler}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{For finite incidence data with positive edge weights #{w(e)>0}, the
\vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007} #{L=B^TWB}
is symmetric and positive semidefinite. Its kernel consists exactly of
fields constant on each connected component of the underlying undirected
graph. Here two vertices lie in the same component when a finite path
of edges joins them, with edge orientation ignored. If there are #{k}
components, then
##{\dim\ker L=k.}
The energy convention and the connected-graph kernel argument appear in
\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}; the proof below also
allows disconnected carriers, loops and parallel edges.}

\proof{
\p{Since #{W^T=W}, one has #{L^T=L}. For vertex fields #{f,g}, the
counting-adjoint identity gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\langle f,Lg\rangle_V\\
&\quad=\langle Bf,WB g\rangle_E\\
&\quad=\sum_e w(e)(Df)(e)(Dg)(e).
\end{aligned}}
Taking #{g=f} yields the energy identity
##{\begin{aligned}
&\langle f,Lf\rangle_V\\
&\quad=\sum_e w(e)(Df)(e)^2\ge0.
\end{aligned}}
Each member of #{E} occurs once. There is no factor #{1/2} in this
\vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004}; an action defined as half of it uses a separate convention.}

\p{If #{Lf=0}, the sum of nonnegative terms is zero. Since #{w(e)>0},
every edge has #{f(h(e))=f(s(e))}. These equalities propagate along any
unoriented path, so #{f} is constant on each component. Conversely, a
componentwise constant field has #{Bf=0}, hence #{Lf=0}.
The indicators of the nonempty components have disjoint supports and are
linearly independent. Every componentwise constant field is the sum of
these indicators multiplied by its component values. They therefore form
a basis of the kernel, proving the dimension statement.}
}

\p{If #{V} is nonempty and connected, the kernel is the one-dimensional
space spanned by the constant field #{\mathbf1_V}. Its positive unit mode is
##{|V|^{-1/2}\mathbf1_V.}
An isolated vertex contributes
one component and one kernel basis vector. If #{V} is empty, the maps
#{s,h:E\to V} force #{E} to be empty too: both the component count and
kernel dimension are zero. Loops contribute zero energy, while distinct
parallel edges each contribute their own positive summand.}

\p{For the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), put #{u=1+t}.
Direct multiplication gives
##{L_t=\begin{pmatrix}
u&-1&0&-t\\
-1&u&-t&0\\
0&-t&u&-1\\
-t&0&-1&u
\end{pmatrix}.}
For #{f=(a,b,c,d)}, the energy is
##{\begin{aligned}
f^TL_tf={}&(b-a)^2+t(c-b)^2\\
          &+(d-c)^2+t(a-d)^2.
\end{aligned}}
Every row of #{L_t} sums to zero, and for #{t>0} zero energy forces all
four values equal. The precision of the
[positive quadratic action](fdip-0004) is exactly
#{Q_{t,m}=L_t+m^2I}. Thus its action is half this edge energy plus the
mass term #{m^2\langle f,f\rangle_V/2}, in agreement with its displayed
matrix and squared-difference formula.}

\p{The hypotheses have concrete consequences. With vertices
#{0,1,2,3} and just the unit-weight edges #{0\to1} and #{2\to3}, the
matrix has two diagonal blocks #{\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)}.
Its kernel is #{\{(a,a,b,b):a,b\in\mathbb R\}}; connectivity is
needed for a single constant mode.}

\p{On two vertices with one edge #{0\to1} of weight zero, the underlying
graph is connected but #{L=0}, so its kernel is all of #{\mathbb R^2}.
For nonnegative weights the same proof identifies the kernel using only
the positive-weight edges. A zero weight also makes the proposed edge
pairing degenerate. If instead this edge has weight #{-1}, then
##{f^TLf=-(f(1)-f(0))^2,}
which is negative at #{f=(0,1)}. Positivity fails, and the edge form is
not an inner product. This example is negative semidefinite; two disjoint
edges with weights #{1} and #{-1} give an indefinite form, since fields
supported on the separate edges have energies of opposite signs.}

\p{For unweighted simple graphs, Mathlib's
\leanref{SimpleGraph.posSemidef_lapMatrix} proves positivity of its
Laplacian. That model has no separate weighted edge set; its energy
formula sums both adjacency directions and divides by two. The
edge-once sum above also handles loops and parallel edges, and is proved
directly here.}
